Q: How can I prevent duplicate WooCommerce webhook processing when the same event is delivered multiple times? A: <p>The check itself is not the hard part, where you keep the flag is. In WordPress the options and transients API looks atomic and is not: <code>add_option()</code> reads the options cache before it writes, so two PHP workers can both see &quot;not there&quot; and both write. With an external object cache the read can also be a stale hit.</p>
<p>Make the INSERT itself the lock and let the unique key pick the winner:</p>
<pre><code>global $wpdb;
$table = $wpdb-&gt;prefix . 'myplugin_webhook_events';

$claimed = $wpdb-&gt;query( $wpdb-&gt;prepare(
    &quot;INSERT IGNORE INTO {$table} (event_id, status, created_at)
     VALUES (%s, 'processing', UTC_TIMESTAMP())&quot;,
    $event_id
) );

if ( ! $claimed ) {
    return new WP_REST_Response( array( 'duplicate' =&gt; true ), 200 );
}

process_order_event( $payload );
$wpdb-&gt;update( $table, array( 'status' =&gt; 'done' ), array( 'event_id' =&gt; $event_id ) );
</code></pre>
<p>with <code>event_id</code> as the primary key on an InnoDB table. The second request loses on the key and never reaches your handler, no matter how close together the two deliveries arrive.</p>
<p>Keep a <code>status</code> column rather than a bare &quot;seen&quot; row: if PHP dies mid-processing, a row stuck in <code>processing</code> longer than your timeout tells you the event was claimed but never finished, so you can reprocess it instead of losing it silently.</p>
<p>And return the 200 quickly, doing the real work through Action Scheduler. A handler slow enough for the sender to time out is exactly what produces the duplicate delivery you are defending against. A hash of the payload works as the id too, but only if the sender does not put a timestamp or a nonce in the body, otherwise the same event hashes differently on each retry.</p>

Q: Trying to get the simplest way of making this number table constructor to work... in Lua A: <p>And, FTR or just in case someone found it useful, this is a kind of richer/improved function-version without loop/limit:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="//github.com/fengari-lua/fengari-web/releases/download/v0.1.4/fengari-web.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type="application/lua"&gt;

function SupOrSub(number, sub) -- (int, bool) char
    local numbers = sub and {"₀","₁","₂","₃","₄","₅","₆","₇","₈","₉"} or {"⁰","¹","²","³","⁴","⁵","⁶","⁷","⁸","⁹"}
    return (tostring(math.max(0, math.floor(tonumber(number) or 0))):gsub("%d", function(d) return numbers[tonumber(d) + 1] end))
end -- E.g.: SupOrSub(0), SupOrSub(2) .. "⁄" .. SupOrSub(8, true), SupOrSub(2) .. "¯" .. SupOrSub(8), SupOrSub(2) .. "⋅" .. SupOrSub(8)

print("&gt; ", SupOrSub(0), SupOrSub(18, true), SupOrSub(960), SupOrSub(2) .. "⁄" .. SupOrSub(8, true), SupOrSub(2) .. "¯" .. SupOrSub(8), SupOrSub(2) .. "⋅" .. SupOrSub(8), SupOrSub(962496843) .. "⋅⋅⋅")

&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>From this <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/75560964/trying-to-get-the-simplest-way-of-making-this-number-table-constructor-to-work#comment141140996_79876058">comment</a>. No &quot;Add Another Answer&quot; button here, so using the &quot;Answer Your Question&quot; one... I hope it's OK.</p>
